# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Reference set for genre `topology` (EXPERIMENTAL).
figdown 0.1 topology
title "Reference: topology fabric"

class uplink "Fabric uplink" stroke=#2563eb

group spine "Spine"
node s1 "S1" shape=rounded in=spine
node s2 "S2" shape=rounded in=spine

group leaf "Leaf"
node l1 "L1" in=leaf
node l2 "L2" in=leaf

external wan "WAN"

flow down
rank s1,s2
rank l1,l2

edge s1 [e0/1] -- [e0/1] l1 class=uplink
edge s1 [e0/2] -- [e0/1] l2
edge s2 [e0/1] -- [e0/2] l1
edge s2 [e0/2] -- [e0/2] l2
edge l1 -> wan
edge l1 -- l2

# A SINGLE-MEMBER bundle (backlog 53). It is legal and it means
# something real — a one-link LAG, an Ethernet Segment with one member — so the
# declaration stands. What changed is the DRAWING: a ring exists to unite lines,
# and around one line it unites nothing, so a one-member bundle now draws its
# CAPTION beside the link and no ellipse.
bundle mh "multi-home" l1--l2
# The MULTI-MEMBER form: the member list is ONE comma-delimited token, and each
# member must resolve to a unique edge, as written (`s1--l1` is not `l1--s1`).
# It was demonstrated in block-experimental.fd until 0.3, when `SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP`
# withdrew `bundle` from `block`; `topology` is now its only genre, so the form
# is demonstrated here. `topology` is also where the word can be defined by its
# REFERENT — an IEEE 802.1AX LAG, an ECMP set, an EVPN Ethernet Segment — which
# is what L1's two spine uplinks are.
bundle ecmp "ECMP uplink set" s1--l1,s2--l1
# The WRITTEN-EMPTY label (`EMPTY-LABEL-STATE`): absent is omitted, `""` is a written value —
# a third state, and the ring drew with no caption rather than falling back to
# the id. This form was demonstrated on a `block` bundle until 0.3; it
# moved here with the keyword, because `topology` is the only genre that
# declares it now.
#
# THIS DECLARATION IS NOW MODEL-ONLY, AND DELIBERATELY KEPT (
# backlog 53). It is a SINGLE-member bundle, so it draws no ring, and its label
# is written-empty, so it draws no caption: the figure shows nothing for it.
# The line is not deleted, because it is the DEMONSTRATOR for the written-empty
# label on `bundle` — the model still carries the trunk, `read` still reports
# it, and a reader who asks the source what s2--l2 is gets an answer. Nothing a
# ring around one unnamed line could draw is a fact the figure did not already
# state, which is precisely why it now draws nothing.
bundle unlabelled "" s2--l2

# WITHDRAWN — `plane tunnels "VXLAN overlay" z-index=1` stood here. `plane`, its
# `z-index=` and the `plane=` that referenced one left the LANGUAGE
# (`PAINT-ORDER-CONSTRUCT`), with no replacement spelling. Of the four scene genres this one had the
# worst collision: in networking a plane is the control/data/management partition
# of a device, and `topology` is the genre network engineers author in — so the
# line above read as an architectural claim and in fact set a paint order.
# A layering relation, if it ever earns a keyword, has a reserved word waiting:
# `network` (RFC 8345 §4.1/§6.1). Today the one figure that wanted it is served
# by `class` (`RESERVED-SPELLINGS`).

# The peer group earns its place in the subject (maintainer review,
# 0.4): it is the spine's eBGP peering, reached by one real edge, so
# the figure reads as one network rather than a catalogue with a loose corner.
# It still carries its three demonstrators: `gap=` on the group, a node whose
# dashed look is now a CLASSED meaning rather than bare presentation, and a
# direct stroke= on the node showing the per-node override beside the class.
class prov "Provisional peer — planned, not yet carrying traffic" style=dashed
group peer "eBGP peers" gap=4
node p1 "Peer 1" in=peer class=prov stroke=#64748b
node p2 "Peer 2" in=peer
edge s2 -[eBGP]- p1

layout
pin s1 at=(80,40) width=72 height=40
pin s2 at=(220,40) width=72 height=40
